## 2026-08-28 (cont.) — deployed
- Both Vercel and Render are now linked to `SumedhaKar/HCLTech` and deployed. Gotcha: the user's Vercel/Render logins are on a *different* GitHub identity (`SwapnilDey-git`) than the one that owns this repo (`SumedhaKar`) — same person, two GitHub accounts. Had to authorize each platform's GitHub App from the `SumedhaKar` side specifically before the repo showed up in the import picker.
- Render build broke twice on Python version: it defaults to the newest Python (3.14.3) regardless of a `backend/runtime.txt` file — that file is apparently only honored at the literal git-repo root, not a service's configured Root Directory (`backend`), so it never took effect. `pydantic-core==2.27.2` has no prebuilt wheel for 3.14 yet, falls back to a `maturin`/cargo source build, which fails outright since Render's build sandbox is read-only outside specific paths. Fix that actually worked: set the `PYTHON_VERSION=3.12.7` env var directly in the Render dashboard (not a repo file) and redeploy. `backend/runtime.txt` is still committed but is dead weight for this platform — env var is what's actually pinning the version.
- Live URLs: frontend `https://hcl-tech-delta.vercel.app`, backend `https://hcltech-73xc.onrender.com`.
- Render env vars set: `DATABASE_URL` (asyncpg pooler string), `GEMINI_API_KEY`, `CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,https://hcl-tech-delta.vercel.app`. Vercel env vars set: `DATABASE_URL` (plain pooler string).
- Full smoke test against the deployed services (not just local): Vercel `/`, `/catalog`, `/api/learner-profile`, `/api/learning-path` all 200 with real Supabase data; Render `/health`, `/docs`, `/recommendations/generate` all 200 with a real Gemini-backed path generation; CORS preflight from the Render backend correctly allows the deployed Vercel origin.
- Note: the FE doesn't call the BE FastAPI service directly from the browser yet (no chat/recommendation UI page built — `/catalog` only calls its own Next.js API route). So CORS hasn't been exercised by real app traffic yet, only by the manual preflight check above — worth a real check once a FE page actually calls the BE client-side.
- Test learner-profile/path data (from earlier local verification pass) is intentionally left in place per the user ("no keep it") — the mock learner now has two generated paths (one from local testing, one from the Render smoke test above), both pointing at the same first course. Harmless for a single-learner demo app, but worth knowing if path history/duplicates ever become visible in a UI.

## 2026-08-29 — homepage design (via /impeccable)
- No visual identity existed before this (homepage was still the unmodified create-next-app scaffold). Wrote PRODUCT.md, then ran the full new-work direction process for the homepage: rolled and landed on "Trailhead Signage" (trail markers, graded difficulty blazes, topographic routes) over five dealt challengers (closest contender was a LEGO-instruction-manual world — wins on pure step-sequencing but loses on aspirational tone and has no difficulty-grading equivalent).
- Built frontend/app/page.tsx (hero + example route + CTA), updated layout.tsx (Zilla Slab/Archivo/JetBrains Mono via next/font/google, direction-contract comment) and globals.css (full color-token system). New frontend/app/RouteWaypoint.tsx for the one authored motion moment.
- Real bugs caught during inspection: (1) initial mobile/desktop screenshots via `chrome --headless=new --window-size=...` were unreliable on this machine — Windows DPI scaling made Chrome report a different `window.innerWidth` than the requested size, and the CLI flag doesn't reliably force it; switched to `puppeteer-core` with explicit `page.setViewport()` for verification, which is exact. (2) Several real WCAG contrast failures (computed by hand): CTA button text 3.2:1, waypoint badge numerals 2.36:1/3.13:1, faint kicker/footer text 3.7:1 — all below 4.5:1. Fixed by darkening the button/badge background colors and lightening the faint text token. (3) An IntersectionObserver-gated scroll-reveal (opacity:0 until scrolled into view) made the entire 5-item route list invisible in one capture mode when the observer never fired — replaced with a pure-CSS `@keyframes` entrance (`.rise-in` in globals.css) that has no JS dependency and always reaches visible within ~1.2s regardless of anything else. General lesson: don't gate real content's visibility behind JS/observer state, even for "nice to have" motion.
- Finish review and DESIGN.md documentation were run in-thread (no `impeccable-finish-reviewer`/`impeccable-documenter` subagent type available in this environment) — disclosed as a substitution rather than silently skipped. Review caught the kicker/eyebrow pattern (banned outright by the craft floor) used twice; both removed.
- DESIGN.md + .impeccable/design.json written from the built result. North star: "The Trailhead Signpost." Catalog page and the chat-intake/learning-path-dashboard UI are still unstyled (next surface to design, per the user's "both, homepage first" answer).

## 2026-08-29 (cont.) — catalog restyle + chat/dashboard UI
- Restyled frontend/app/catalog/page.tsx to the Trailhead Signage system (no new direction roll needed — extending an established world, same reasoning as any "restyle, don't reinvent" pass). Added a shared frontend/app/components/SiteHeader.tsx used by catalog/chat/dashboard.
- Built the two screens the product had zero UI for: frontend/app/chat/page.tsx (chat-intake, structure chosen via a real concept-seed roll — assigned "chat thread + live trail-permit checklist," confirmed by the user over 3 alternates) and frontend/app/dashboard/page.tsx (learning-path dashboard, deliberately reused DESIGN.md's own pinned waypoint-marker language rather than re-rolling structure, since DESIGN.md already commits future sequence/progress UI to that pattern). New frontend/app/lib/backend.ts + `NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_URL` env var so the frontend can call FastAPI directly from the browser for the first time (chat intake, recommendation generation, explainer) — previously only server-side Next.js API routes existed.
- Full functional verification, not just visual: reinstalled `puppeteer-core` temporarily (removed after), drove a real multi-turn conversation against the live local backend + live Gemini, clicked through to real path generation, toggled real status changes, asked the real explainer a question. All genuinely works end to end.
- Real bugs found and fixed in this pass: (1) local dev must be tested via `http://localhost:3000`, not `127.0.0.1:3000` — Next 16's dev-origin check silently breaks the HMR websocket and some client interactivity on the IP form, which looked exactly like a broken submit button until diagnosed. (2) The "Generate my path" button was gated on the backend's `profileComplete` flag, which can be `true` while the frontend's own checklist still shows a field unfilled — changed the gate to the frontend's own all-four-fields-known check so the UI never contradicts what it's showing the user, independent of backend correctness. (3) Two craft-floor repeats: a kicker-label-above-heading on the dashboard (same banned pattern already fixed once on the homepage — worth double-checking new surfaces against this specific rule going forward) and a shadow value that drifted slightly from DESIGN.md's own "Two-Shadow Rule" on the chat panel; both fixed to match the system exactly.
- **Real, unfixed backend bug found via this end-to-end testing** (out of scope for this UI pass, needs its own fix): `backend/app/services/gemini_intake.py`'s `experienceLevel` field almost never gets populated by the live Gemini call, even when a user states it as explicitly as possible ("I am a complete beginner with no programming experience at all" directly answering "what is your experience level?" still comes back `experienceLevel: null`). Worse, in the same test the model fabricated `timeBudgetHoursPerWeek: 5` out of nowhere (never mentioned, directly violating the prompt's own "never guess or invent values" rule), and returned `profileComplete: true` despite its own stated completion rule (goal + experience level + interest) not being satisfied. All three symptoms came from one direct `curl` test against the live endpoint, not inferred. Likely candidates: something in how `google-genai==0.3.0` handles the `enum`-constrained schema field, or a live-model behavior issue — needs actual debugging, not a guess-fix. This blocks a real user from ever completing the chat-intake flow if experienceLevel truly never extracts; the frontend fix above (gate on all 4 fields) makes this visible/honest rather than silently broken, but doesn't fix the underlying extraction.

## Open, not yet resolved
- **`gemini_intake.py` experienceLevel/hallucination bug above — real, reproducible, blocks the chat-intake flow from ever completing for real users.** Needs backend debugging (not a UI task).
- Otherwise nothing blocking. Full stack (Supabase + Render + Vercel) is live; Vercel/Render don't yet have `NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_URL` set for the new chat/dashboard screens to work in production (currently only wired for local dev) — Vercel needs this added as an env var pointing at the Render URL.
- Recurring pattern worth naming: every Gemini-calling piece of code written *before* a live key existed has had stale model names and/or the schema-as-dict issue. If more AI-calling code gets written blind (no live key), assume it needs the same pass once it can finally be tested for real.
